---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import Support from "~/components/Support.astro";
import { candidates, categories, formatDate } from "~/lib/data";

const withStatement = candidates.filter((c) => c.sources.length > 0);
const withPositions = candidates.filter((c) => c.positions.length > 0);
const quoted = candidates.reduce((n, c) => n + c.positions.length, 0);
const statementOnly = withStatement.filter((c) => c.positions.length === 0);
const retrieved = withStatement[0]?.sources[0]?.retrieved;
---

<Base
  title="How this was built"
  description="Sources, tagging method, limitations and corrections policy for North Van Votes."
>
  <h1>How this was built</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This page exists so you can check the site rather than trust it. It covers
    where every quote came from, how topics were assigned, what the method
    misses, and how to get something fixed.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>{candidates.length}</b><span>candidates</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{withStatement.length}</b><span>published a statement</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{withPositions.length}</b><span>have a quoted position</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{quoted}</b><span>quoted passages</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the words come from</h2>
  <p>
    Every quote is taken from a candidate's own official statement, filed with
    their municipality by the nomination deadline and published unedited by that
    municipality:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.cnv.org/City-Hall/General-Local-Election/2026-General-Local-Election/Candidate-Profiles" rel="noopener">
        City of North Vancouver — Candidate Profiles
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.dnv.org/government-administration/see-who-is-running" rel="noopener">
        District of North Vancouver — See who is running
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Statements were retrieved on {retrieved && formatDate(retrieved)}. Neither
    municipality verifies the accuracy of what candidates write, and neither does
    this site. Contact details, photos, nomination documents and campaign links
    come from the same two pages.
  </p>

  <h2>How quotes are guaranteed to be verbatim</h2>
  <p>This is the part that matters most, so it is worth being precise about.</p>
  <p>
    Topic tagging is done by a language model, but the model is never allowed to
    produce text. Each statement is split into numbered sentences, the model is
    shown those numbered sentences, and it replies with <em>sentence
    numbers</em> only. The site's own code then cuts those exact characters out
    of the stored source document.
  </p>
  <p>
    That means a paraphrase or an invented quote is not something the process
    tries to detect — it is something the process cannot produce. Every passage
    is additionally re-checked against its source before publishing, and any
    passage that does not match character-for-character is dropped rather than
    shown. The worst error the method can make is quoting a real sentence under a
    topic you would not have filed it under.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No ratings, scores, grades or endorsements.</strong> Every candidate
      gets identical fields and identical space.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No conclusions from silence.</strong> Where a candidate has not
      addressed a topic, the site says the sources checked did not cover it. That
      is a fact about our reading, not a judgement about them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No alphabetical advantage.</strong> Candidates within a race are
      listed in a randomised order that changes per browsing session. You can
      switch to A–Z with the button on any list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No tracking.</strong> No analytics, no cookies, no accounts. Your
      shortlist, if you make one, stays in your own browser.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Candidate statements are short — a few hundred words. A candidate with
      detailed policy on their own website may look thinner here than they are.
      Their campaign link is always shown; follow it.
    </li>
    <li>
      {statementOnly.length} candidates published a statement that sets out no
      position on any tracked topic — usually because it is biography rather than
      policy. They are listed as such, not as silent.
    </li>
    <li>
      The {categories.length} topics are a starting set drawn from what
      candidates themselves wrote about most. They are not an official list and
      they are not exhaustive.
    </li>
    <li>
      One sentence can legitimately belong to two topics, so the same quote
      sometimes appears in two places.
    </li>
    <li>
      Nothing here is scraped from news coverage, all-candidates meetings, social
      media or interviews. Those are real sources of candidates' views; they are
      simply outside what this site claims to cover.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If a quote misrepresents you, a topic is wrong, a link is broken, or
    something on the voting page is out of date, say so and it will be fixed
    within 48 hours — or the item will be removed while it is checked.
    Candidates, campaigns and readers are all equally welcome to ask.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:corrections@northvanvotes.ca">corrections@northvanvotes.ca</a>
  </p>
  <p class="candidate-meta">
    Corrections that are a matter of record — a wrong link, a mis-transcribed
    date — are simply fixed. A request to remove or reword an accurate quotation
    from a published statement will be declined, with the reason given; the
    statement is a public document and the quote will keep pointing at it.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>
    North Van Votes is not affiliated with any candidate, campaign, elector
    organization, political party, municipality or school district. It is not
    funded by, and accepts nothing from, anyone running in this election or
    anyone advocating for or against them. It carries no advertising.
  </p>

  <Support variant="block" />

  <div class="notice">
    <p>
      <strong>Regulatory status.</strong> BC's Local Elections Campaign Financing
      Act governs third-party election advertising, and during the campaign
      period that definition reaches communications taking a position on an issue
      a candidate is associated with.
    </p>
    <p>
      Elections BC's own
      {" "}<a href="https://elections.bc.ca/docs/lecfa/4500003-Local-election-advertising-quick-reference-sheet.pdf" rel="noopener">
      advertising quick reference sheet</a> lists "websites or blogs" and "any
      free communication on the internet" under <em>not</em> election
      advertising. The line it draws is placement cost — a newspaper ad, a
      billboard, a paid social post, a robocall. This site has no placement cost,
      carries no advertising, is never paid to be promoted, and neither promotes
      nor opposes any candidate.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Elections BC can be reached at 1-800-661-8683 or
      {" "}<a href="mailto:electoral.finance@elections.bc.ca">electoral.finance@elections.bc.ca</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Built on the open record</h2>
  <p>
    The whole thing is a small pipeline that downloads public documents, caches
    them, extracts quotes, and renders static pages. The cached source documents
    are kept alongside the code so the dataset can be rebuilt and checked by
    anyone.
  </p>
</Base>
